    List<User> findByAgeGreaterThan(int age);
}
```

With Query DSL, you can create more complex queries using the attributes of the `User` entity. Here's how you would do it:

First, you need to add the Querydsl dependency to your `pom.xml` or `build.gradle`:

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>com.querydsl</groupId>
    <artifactId>querydsl-jpa</artifactId>
</dependency>
```

Then, you need to enable Querydsl support in your Spring Boot application:

```java
@Configuration
@EnableJpaRepositories(basePackages = "com.example.repository")
@EnableQuerydsl
public class QuerydslConfiguration {
    // additional configurations if needed
}
```

Now, you can create a query using Query DSL:

```java
import static com.example.entity.QUser.user;

@Repository
public interface UserRepository extends JpaRepository<User, Long>, QuerydslPredicateExecutor<User> {
    default List<User> findByAgeGreaterThan(int age) {
        JPAQuery<User> query = new JPAQuery<>(getEntityManager());
        return query.select(user)
                    .from(user)
                    .where(user.age.gt(age))
                    .fetch();
    }
}
```

In this example, `QUser` is a generated Querydsl class based on the `User` entity. With Query DSL, you can use `QUser` to refer to entity attributes (`user.age.gt(age)`) instead of using raw attribute names (`user.getAge()`), providing type safety and compile-time checks.

