To generate the next date based on a given start date, end date, and recurrence using Java Spring Boot, you can follow these steps:

1. Parse the start and end date strings into `LocalDate` objects.
2. Calculate the next date based on the recurrence.
3. Ensure that the next date is within the specified range (between start and end dates).

Here's a Spring Boot program to achieve this:

```java
import org.springframework.boot.SpringApplication;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.SpringBootApplication;
import java.time.LocalDate;
import java.time.format.DateTimeFormatter;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

@SpringBootApplication
public class DateGeneratorApplication {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(DateGeneratorApplication.class, args);

        String startDateStr = "01-Jan-2023"; // Replace with your start date string
        String endDateStr = "31-Dec-2023";   // Replace with your end date string
        LocalDate startDate = parseDate(startDateStr);
        LocalDate endDate = parseDate(endDateStr);

        Recurrence recurrence = Recurrence.MONTHLY; // Replace with your desired recurrence

        LocalDate nextDate = calculateNextDate(startDate, recurrence);
        while (!nextDate.isAfter(endDate)) {
            System.out.println(nextDate.format(DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("dd-MMM-yyyy")));
            nextDate = calculateNextDate(nextDate, recurrence);
        }
    }

    private static LocalDate parseDate(String dateStr) {
        DateTimeFormatter formatter = DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("dd-MMM-yyyy");
        return LocalDate.parse(dateStr, formatter);
    }

    private static LocalDate calculateNextDate(LocalDate currentDate, Recurrence recurrence) {
        switch (recurrence) {
            case DAILY:
                return currentDate.plusDays(1);
            case WEEKLY:
                return currentDate.plusWeeks(1);
            case MONTHLY:
                return currentDate.plusMonths(1);
            case QUARTERLY:
                return currentDate.plusMonths(3);
            case HALF_YEARLY:
                return currentDate.plusMonths(6);
            case YEARLY:
                return currentDate.plusYears(1);
            default:
                throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unsupported recurrence: " + recurrence);
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, the program starts with a given start date and iteratively calculates and prints the next date based on the specified recurrence until it reaches the end date. You can change the start date, end date, and recurrence as needed for your use case.

