It seems like the WebSocket server is treating each new browser instance as a separate client, hence logging "Client disconnected" whenever you open a new browser. This is because WebSocket connections are made on a per-client basis.

If you want to maintain a persistent connection for each user across multiple browser instances, you can use some form of authentication to identify users uniquely. Here's a modified approach to achieve this:

1. **Use JWT for Authentication**: Implement JWT (JSON Web Tokens) authentication. When a user logs in, generate a JWT token and send it to the client. The client can then include this token in the WebSocket connection request to identify itself.

2. **Associate WebSocket Connection with User**: When a WebSocket connection is established, validate the JWT token sent by the client to identify the user. Associate the WebSocket connection with the user so that you can send messages to the correct user.

3. **Handle User Logout**: When a user logs out or closes the browser, close the WebSocket connection associated with that user.

Here's a high-level overview of how you can modify your server to implement JWT authentication:

```javascript
// server.js
const jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');

// Function to generate JWT token
const generateToken = (username) => {
  return jwt.sign({ username }, 'your_secret_key', { expiresIn: '1h' });
};

// Middleware to verify JWT token
const verifyToken = (token) => {
  try {
    const decoded = jwt.verify(token, 'your_secret_key');
    return decoded.username;
  } catch (err) {
    return null;
  }
};

wss.on('connection', (ws, req) => {
  const token = req.headers.authorization;
  const username = verifyToken(token);
  if (!username) {
    ws.close();
    return;
  }
  console.log(`${username} connected`);

  // Handle messages, etc.
});
```

In this setup, the client needs to include the JWT token in the `Authorization` header when connecting via WebSocket. The server then verifies the token to identify the user.

This approach ensures that each user has a persistent WebSocket connection across multiple browser instances. When a user logs out or closes the browser, you can close the WebSocket connection associated with that user.

To display all logged-in users in your Chat component using WebSocket, you need to establish a WebSocket connection from your React frontend to the WebSocket server we created earlier. Then, you can send a request to the server to retrieve the list of logged-in users and update the state of your Chat component accordingly. Here's how you can do it:

1. In your Chat component, you can establish a WebSocket connection and send a request to the server to get the list of logged-in users. Update the `Chat.js` component:

```jsx
// src/Chat.js
import React, { useEffect, useState } from 'react';
import { useSelector } from 'react-redux';
import { selectUsername } from './features/userSlice';

const Chat = () => {
  const username = useSelector(selectUsername);
  const [loggedInUsers, setLoggedInUsers] = useState([]);

  useEffect(() => {
    const ws = new WebSocket('ws://localhost:5000');

    ws.onopen = () => {
      console.log('WebSocket connected');
      ws.send(JSON.stringify({ type: 'getLoggedInUsers' }));
    };

    ws.onmessage = (event) => {
      const data = JSON.parse(event.data);
      if (data.type === 'loggedInUsers') {
        setLoggedInUsers(data.users);
      }
    };

    return () => {
      ws.close();
    };
  }, []);

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Welcome, {username}!</h2>
      <h3>Logged-in Users:</h3>
      <ul>
        {loggedInUsers.map((user, index) => (
          <li key={index}>{user}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  );
};

export default Chat;
```

2. Modify the WebSocket server to handle the 'getLoggedInUsers' request and send the list of logged-in users to the client:

```javascript
// server.js
const express = require('express');
const http = require('http');
const WebSocket = require('ws');

const app = express();
const server = http.createServer(app);
const wss = new WebSocket.Server({ server });

const loggedInUsers = [];

wss.on('connection', (ws) => {
  console.log('Client connected');

  ws.on('message', (message) => {
    const data = JSON.parse(message);
    if (data.type === 'login') {
      const { username } = data;
      loggedInUsers.push(username);
      console.log(`${username} logged in.`);
    } else if (data.type === 'getLoggedInUsers') {
      ws.send(JSON.stringify({ type: 'loggedInUsers', users: loggedInUsers }));
    }
  });

  ws.on('close', () => {
    console.log('Client disconnected');
  });
});

app.use(express.json());

app.post('/login', (req, res) => {
  const { username } = req.body;
  loggedInUsers.push(username);
  res.status(200).json({ message: 'Login successful' });
});

app.get('/logged-in-users', (req, res) => {
  res.status(200).json(loggedInUsers);
});

const PORT = process.env.PORT || 5000;

server.listen(PORT, () => {
  console.log(`Server started on port ${PORT}`);
});
```

With these changes, your Chat component will now display the list of logged-in users obtained from the WebSocket server. When the Chat component mounts, it establishes a WebSocket connection, sends a request to get the list of logged-in users, and updates the state accordingly when the response is received.

