Then, you can create your GraphQL schema (`schema.graphqls`):

```graphql
type Query {
    add(a: Int!, b: Int!): Int
    greet(name: String!): String
}
```

Next, create resolvers for your schema:

```java
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import com.coxautodev.graphql.tools.GraphQLQueryResolver;

@Component
public class QueryResolver implements GraphQLQueryResolver {
    public Integer add(Integer a, Integer b) {
        return a + b;
    }

    public String greet(String name) {
        return "Hello " + name;
    }
}
```

Finally, configure your GraphQL servlet and endpoint in your Spring Boot application:

```java
import org.springframework.boot.SpringApplication;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.SpringBootApplication;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import com.graphql.kickstart.tools.SchemaParser;
import com.graphql.kickstart.tools.SchemaParserOptions;
import com.graphql.kickstart.tools.SchemaParserBuilder;
import graphql.GraphQL;
import graphql.schema.GraphQLSchema;

@SpringBootApplication
public class MyApplication {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(MyApplication.class, args);
    }

    @Bean
    public GraphQLSchema schema(QueryResolver queryResolver) {
        SchemaParserOptions options = SchemaParserOptions.newOptions().build();
        SchemaParser schemaParser = new SchemaParser(options);
        GraphQLSchema schema = schemaParser.makeExecutableSchema(
                SchemaParserBuilder.newParser()
                        .files("schema.graphqls")
                        .resolvers(queryResolver)
                        .build());
        return schema;
    }

    @Bean
    public GraphQL graphQL(GraphQLSchema schema) {
        return GraphQL.newGraphQL(schema).build();
    }
}
```

This sets up a simple Spring Boot application with two GraphQL endpoints. The `add` endpoint takes two integers and returns their sum, while the `greet` endpoint takes a name and returns a greeting.
